atter{\n                \"status\":       \"in_progress\",\n                \"target_vault\": 42,\n            },\n        }\n        err := publisher.PublishSpawnNotification(ctx, task, \"claude-20260418120000\")\n        Expect(err).NotTo(HaveOccurred())\n        _, cmd := decodeUpdateFrontmatterCommand(producer.messages[0])\n        Expect(cmd.TargetVault).To(Equal(\"\"))\n    })\n})\n```\n\nWait — the requirement says \"returning \"\" when absent or not a string\". The non-string case is an important edge case to test (per project rules: test all edge cases). Good.\n\nBut note: in the existing test suite, the `PublishTypeMismatchFailure` test uses a task with `assignee: \"agent-pr-reviewer\"`. In my \"all constructions\" test I use the same assignee so `previous_assignee` will be set — that's fine, I'm only asserting TargetVault.\n\nOne thing to double check: does `PublishSetTriggerScope` have any dedupe or validation that might interfere? No. Does PublishFailure in my first test work? Yes.\n\nAlso, I want to double-check: for the \"absent\" test, is it truly \"field absent on the wire\"? The omitempty means when TargetVault == \"\", JSON omits \"targetVault\". When decoded, cmd.TargetVault == \"\". My assertion `Expect(updateCmd.TargetVault).To(Equal(\"\"))` verifies the decode. To make it more explicit about \"absent on wire\", I could decode the raw data into a map and check. But that would require more setup. Let me add a lightweight wire-level check: re-marshal the decoded command and confirm no \"targetVault\" key. Actually simpler: the decoded struct field empty + omitempty comment is adequate and matches the existing test style (which asserts on decoded structs, not raw wire).\n\nActually, let me add a small helper to check the wire absence directly, to be faithful to the requirement \"(field absent on the wire)\". I can do:\n\n```go\nraw, err := producer.messages[0].Value.Encode()\nExpect(err).NotTo(HaveOccurred())\nvar command base.Command\nExpect(json